{"text": "The market-risk picture is incomplete on the one-year comparison the way the desk’s price methods are built. The desk settled JPM’s current 30-day volatility, beta to SPY, and distance from the 52-week high, but it could not settle the twelve-month-ago versions because those price statistics are only expressed on the latest window, not as-of a past date [rep_3d15ad4012b4]. So I can’t say from the desk evidence whether JPM is riskier than a year ago on those three price measures.

On the issuer side, the bank-specific caveat matters: the desk does not apply operating-style issuer methods to a financial issuer, so it will not give bank-style leverage or cash-flow ratios for JPM. What it did give instead is the financing-and-earnings bridge you asked for. Interest expense was $5.55B [f_5139425ad2ae@2021-12-31], $26.10B [f_5139425ad2ae@2022-12-31], and $81.32B [f_5139425ad2ae@2023-12-31]. Pretax income was $61.61B [f_23ebdb449f9f@2023-12-31], $75.08B [f_23ebdb449f9f@2024-12-31], and $72.59B [f_23ebdb449f9f@2025-12-31]; net income was $49.55B [f_c1cbc326193d@2023-12-31], $58.47B [f_c1cbc326193d@2024-12-31], and $57.05B [f_c1cbc326193d@2025-12-31]. The pretax-to-net-income gap was $12.06B [f_5d8f4857f009@2023-12-31], $16.61B [f_5d8f4857f009@2024-12-31], and $15.55B [f_5d8f4857f009@2025-12-31].

What that says for the question: the earnings bridge has been fairly steady over the last three annual points, but the desk does not give you the historical price-risk comparison you need to prove that the market is treating JPM as riskier than a year ago. The right reading from the desk is “not established,” not “yes.”"}

